!<
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Space Hunter Lazy Workflow -- shared content.
% Included by SHLazyWorkflowPart1.pidoc and SHLazyWorkflowPart2.pidoc inside
% their "Requirements and License" section. Do not compile on its own (the
% "!<" line above makes the compiler skip it if you do).
% Checked against Part 1 v2.5 / Part 2 v2.3 TEST, 2026-10-11.
% "Files, Programs and Internet Access" checked against Part 1 v2.5.11 /
% Part 2 v2.3.8, 2026-10-23 (CPD program rules: document external programs,
% network connections and file locations). ASTAP removed for Part 1 v2.6.0,
% 2026-10-30 (CPD rule 5.8): blind solving by ImageSolver 6.6.0.
% Checked against Part 1 v2.7.6 / Part 2 v2.4.6, 2026-11-02 (release review):
% ImageSolver's online catalogs, MARS folder, no SXT->StarNet2 fallback.
% "Files, Programs and Internet Access" completed for Part 1 v2.7.7 / Part 2
% v2.4.8, 2026-11-02 (security review): mark names, HISTORY lines, previews
% deleted, ImageSolver settings and star lists, local paths not in icons,
% vendor tools.
% Portable paths for Part 1 v2.7.24 / Part 2 v2.4.20, 2026-11-09: Windows,
% macOS or Linux; MARS search without fixed folders; XSPD files picked once;
% Session twins + SpaceHunter:SPFCMode listed (CPD: properties written).
% Review fixes for Part 1 v2.7.30 / Part 2 v2.4.25, 2026-11-09: background
% finder deletes all old previews, MAS preview + stars windows, ImageSolver
% settings complete, MARS search order, HISTORY lines of an undone correction.
% Part 1 v2.7.32, 2026-11-17: gradient record = property
% SpaceHunter:GradientModel instead of SH: HISTORY lines.
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection { \label requirements Requirements } {

   \s {Both parts}

   \list[spaced] {
      { Windows, macOS or Linux. The scripts take all file locations from
        PixInsight itself; they have been tested on Windows. }
      { PixInsight 1.9.6 or later. }
      { An image from a \s {one-shot colour (OSC)} camera, captured with a
        broadband, light-pollution or single dual-narrowband (DNB) filter.
        The RGB channels are \e not split into separate greyscale images. }
      { An image that is already cropped. If you stack in WBPP with
        \e {Auto Crop} and \e {Astrometric Solution} enabled, you can start
        without cropping and the plate solve step is skipped. }
   }

   \s {Part 1}

   \list[spaced] {
      { The MARS database (any drive). }
      { For images without coordinates or image scale in their FITS
        keywords: a Gaia astrometric index file from the PixInsight
        Software Distribution, either \c {gdr3-idx-1.0.0-g17.xpsd} (about
        1.8 GB) or \c {gdr3-idx-1.0.0-g19.xpsd} (about 10.3 GB), set as \e {Astrometric Index File} in
        \m {Process > Astrometry > Gaia} preferences. ImageSolver's blind
        solver needs it. }
      { Recommended: the local Gaia DR3 star catalogue (XPSD files), set in
        the same Gaia preferences. Without it, ImageSolver uses an online
        catalogue (see \lref access {Files, Programs and Internet Access}). }
   }

   \s {Part 2}

   \list[spaced] {
      { \xref {https://www.rc-astro.com/} {BlurXTerminator, StarXTerminator
        and NoiseXTerminator}. }
      { Optional: \xref {https://www.starnetastro.com/download/} {StarNet2},
        installed from its PixInsight repository. }
      { Optional: MLDenoise and its model file (\c .xmlm), saved on this
        computer. They are available only to licensed PixInsight users: log
        in on \xref {https://pixinsight.com/} {pixinsight.com} with your
        license to download them. }
      { AutoColor is fully integrated into Part 2. You don't need to install
        or load the AutoColor script separately. (The stand-alone AutoColor
        V8 script is in the
        \xref {https://mopscripts.github.io/mop-v8-scripts/} {MOP V8 scripts
        repository}, if you want to use it on its own.) }
      { Internet access only if you use \e {Check dust map} in the background
        pre-check. }
   }

   \note {
      If Part 1 can't find the MARS database, it asks you for its folder.
      That folder is saved and searched first on later runs; \e {Reset
      Settings} forgets it. Both parts read SPCC's filter and white
      reference files from PixInsight's library folder; if one of them isn't
      there, the script asks you for it once and remembers it. If a tool
      that Part 2 calls (BlurXTerminator,
      StarXTerminator, StarNet2, NoiseXTerminator, MLDenoise) isn't
      installed, that step is skipped with a warning in the console. Select
      StarNet2 instead of StarXTerminator yourself if you use it.
   }

   If you combine two DNB filters (Ha/OIII and SII/OIII) into a Hubble-palette
   image, these scripts are not the right tool.
}

\subsection { \label access Files, Programs and Internet Access } {

   Besides your open images, this is everything the scripts store, write,
   start or download.

   \list[spaced] {
      { \s {Settings:} workflow and equipment settings, the MARS folder you
        picked and the separation-profile folder (Part 1), and the
        MLDenoise model path (Part 2), and a filter or white reference file
        you picked (both parts, only if PixInsight's library folder didn't
        have it) are stored in PixInsight's own settings on this computer.
        \e {Reset Settings} clears the equipment settings and the MARS
        folder; the profile folder, the model path and picked filter files
        are kept. A process icon (\lref buttons {New Instance}) holds the
        workflow and equipment settings, but not these folder and file
        paths. }
      { \s {Re-run marks:} each step leaves a small mark on the image, so a
        second run can skip steps that are still applied. A mark is an XISF
        property holding the step's position in the image's history. Part 1:
        \c {SpaceHunter:SeparationDone}, \c {SpaceHunter:SPFCDone},
        \c {SpaceHunter:GradientDone}. Part 2:
        \c {SpaceHunter:BXTCorrectDone}, \c {SpaceHunter:SPCCDone},
        \c {SpaceHunter:AutoColorDone}, \c {SpaceHunter:BXTFullDone},
        \c {SpaceHunter:SXTDone}, \c {SpaceHunter:StarNet2Done},
        \c {SpaceHunter:BackgroundDone}. The marks are saved only in XISF
        files. Each mark also gets a twin with \c Session added to its name
        (for example \c {SpaceHunter:SPFCDoneSession}). It holds the name of
        the marked process and lets the script check that this step is still
        active in the history. The twins are not saved in image files and are
        gone when you reopen the file. }
      { \s {SPFC filter mode:} after SPFC, Part 1 stores the filter mode it
        used in \c {SpaceHunter:SPFCMode}:
        \c {narrowband: &lt;R&gt;, &lt;G&gt;, &lt;B&gt;} (each as
        wavelength/bandwidth in nm, for example
        \c {narrowband: 656.30/3.00, 500.70/3.00, 500.70/3.00}),
        \c {precombined: &lt;filter set&gt;} or \c {broadband: &lt;filter set&gt;}.
        It is saved in XISF files. Part 2 only reads it, to warn you when
        SPCC uses other filters: another mode (narrowband, a dual-band set
        or a broadband set), another dual-band set, or other narrowband
        wavelengths or bandwidths (more than 0.1 nm apart).
        \c precombined marks a dual-band set. }
      { \s {Gradient record:} when you apply an MGC background model or a GC
        result, Part 1 records which one in the property
        \c {SpaceHunter:GradientModel}: the preview with its gradient scale
        and scale factors (MGC), or its GC settings. Each Apply replaces the
        record. It is saved in XISF files. Part 1 writes no FITS keywords;
        \c HISTORY lines starting with \c {SH:} in older images come from
        versions before Part 1 v2.7.32 and are left as they are. }
      { \s {Previews and windows:} steps 3 and 5 (GC) delete the image's
        existing previews before they create their own. The apply windows
        (steps 4b and 5b) remove their previews, and GC's model windows,
        after applying when that option is ticked. Part 2's background
        finder, once it has found the background, deletes all of the
        image's existing previews (exclusion zones, leftover MGC previews
        and your own) and keeps the one it makes, \c {Background}.
        \s {Show corrected image} in step 4b opens one extra image window,
        \c {SH_corrected_preview}, which closes with the model window and
        is never saved. Part 2's MAS Preview opens a minimised window
        \c {&lt;image&gt;_MAS_preview}, and Part 2 renames the stars image
        made by SXT or StarNet2 to \c stars (\c stars1 and so on if that
        name is taken). }
      { \s {Part 1, ImageSolver:} uses PixInsight's own ImageSolver. It
        starts from the settings you last saved in the ImageSolver script
        (for example the VizieR server and star detection). Part 1 sets only
        the catalogue mode (automatic), the automatic limit magnitude,
        distortion correction, a retry with apparent coordinates, automatic
        PSF fitting (off) and the blind solver, and turns ImageSolver's
        diagnostic outputs off. ImageSolver writes star lists
        (\c {stars*.csv}) to the system temporary folder and leaves them
        there. Its blind solver reads the astrometric index file set in the
        Gaia preferences. }
      { \s {Part 1, ImageSolver star catalogue (internet):} ImageSolver
        uses the local Gaia star catalogue (XPSD) when it is installed. If
        it isn't, or for very bright limit magnitudes (5 or less), ImageSolver
        downloads the stars it needs from an online catalogue (Bright Stars,
        TYCHO-2 or Gaia DR2) through the VizieR service set in ImageSolver's
        preferences. It sends the image's centre coordinates and search
        radius. This is ImageSolver's own behaviour; install the local Gaia
        catalogue to solve offline. }
      { \s {Filter and white reference databases:} both parts read
        \c filters.xspd and \c white-references.xspd from PixInsight's
        library folder, and your own filter file (\c {filters-*-pxi.xspd})
        from PixInsight's settings folder, reading only. }
      { \s {Part 1, MARS:} if you picked a MARS folder in the script, it
        uses the \c .xmars files in that folder together with those in
        PixInsight's library and settings folders. Otherwise it uses the \c .xmars files set in
        MultiscaleGradientCorrection's preferences, and if there are none,
        PixInsight's library and settings folders. Reading only. }
      { \s {Part 1, separation profiles:} profiles are \c .txt files in the
        folder you choose. They are only read for their two factors, never
        run, and written only when you save one (an existing file is
        replaced only after you confirm). }
      { \s {Programs:} the scripts start no external programs. }
      { \s {Part 2, other tools:} BlurXTerminator, StarXTerminator,
        NoiseXTerminator, StarNet2 and MLDenoise are separate products that
        Part 2 runs or opens inside PixInsight. Whether they connect to the
        internet themselves (for example for license or update checks) is up
        to them. }
      { \s {Part 2, Check dust map:} only when you click it, Part 2 sends the
        image's centre coordinates and field size to the NASA/IPAC IRSA dust
        service over https and downloads the dust map from that service only
        to the system temporary folder. The files are deleted afterwards,
        also when a download fails. No other data is sent. If the first
        attempt fails, the script tries once more. Apart from this and
        ImageSolver's online catalogue (above), the scripts don't use the
        internet. }
   }
}

\subsection { \label buttons The Button Bar } {

   \center {
      \image[width:480px] images/Part1_3.png
   }

   The buttons at the bottom of the dialog, from left to right:

   \definition {
      { New Instance }     { Drag the triangle onto the workspace to save
                             the current settings as a process icon. Folder
                             and file paths on this computer are not
                             included. }
      { User Guide }       { Opens the user guide. }
      { Deselect All }     { Clears every workflow step. }
      { Reset Workflow }   { Returns the workflow steps to their defaults.
                             Equipment settings are not changed. }
      { Reset Settings }   { (gear) Returns the equipment settings (camera,
                             filters, white reference, gradient scales) to
                             their defaults. Workflow steps are not changed. }
   }

   Workflow and equipment settings are saved as you change them in the
   dialog, so they are kept even if you click \s {Cancel} (for example to
   look something up first), and come back the next time you open the
   script. \s {Reset Settings} clears the saved equipment settings at once.
   Settings that are damaged or out of range (for example in an edited
   process icon) are replaced by their defaults.
}

